@model ScottNeidig.Web.Models.ServicePageModel
@*
    Business websites page. The least-backed of the three, so it claims the least. Per the
    spec's plan-vs-done rule there is still no client site to point at, so the only example
    named here is this site, which is real and inspectable. The local/Colorado framing is gone
    with the rest of the location signals.

    Do NOT write claims that imply an established practice or clients that don't exist.
*@

<section class="section--lede">
    <div class="container">
        <p class="topper">Business websites</p>
        <h1 class="header">Sites built to be read by search engines</h1>
        <p class="text lede__text">
            Server-rendered pages, no JavaScript framework in the way, and markup a crawler
            can read on the first pass. The site you're on is built that way, and it's the
            clearest example I have.
        </p>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="section--alt">
    <div class="container">
        <p class="topper">The approach</p>
        <h2 class="title">How these are built</h2>

        <ul class="features">
            <li class="feature">
                <h3 class="feature__title">Fast</h3>
                <p class="feature__text">
                    Critical CSS inline-first, deferred everything else, compressed images
                    generated on upload, and close to no JavaScript.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li class="feature">
                <h3 class="feature__title">Readable by crawlers</h3>
                <p class="feature__text">
                    Real HTML from the server, semantic structure, canonical URLs, generated
                    sitemap, and JSON-LD. Nothing that depends on a script running first.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li class="feature">
                <h3 class="feature__title">Editable</h3>
                <p class="feature__text">
                    A small admin behind the site so content, images and posts can be changed
                    without a developer and without a page rebuild.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</section>

@* Proof appears once a category is assigned to this page and has published work in it, so this
   stays empty and honest until there's something real to show. *@
<partial name="_RelatedProjects" model="Model" />
<partial name="_RelatedPosts" model="Model" />
